{{also|épithélium}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|epi-|t1=on, atop|lang2=grc|θηλή|t2=nipple|-ium|t3=aggregation}}, according to Wikipedia due to it being coined for the covering of small "nipples" of tissue on the lip. {{surf|en|epi-|-thelium}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɛpɪˈθiːlɪəm/|a=UK}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːliəm|s=5}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|epithelia}}

# {{lb|en|anatomy}} A membranous tissue composed of one or more layers of cells which forms the covering of most internal and external surfaces of the body and its organs: internally including the lining of vessels and other small cavities, and externally being the skin.
#: {{syn|en|epithelial tissue}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1969|author=Vladimir Nabokov|title=Ada or Ardor|page=25|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2011
|passage=the passenger's roving eyes paused for a moment as he listened inwardly to a nether itch, which he supposed to be (correctly, thank Log) only a minor irritation of the '''epithelium'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2014-05-30|author=Victor Kuete|title=Toxicological Survey of African Medicinal Plants|publisher=Elsevier|isbn=9780128004753|page=505|text=The sap of this species was able to cause loss of corneal '''epithelium''' 16 h after direct exposure to the sap in a 60-year-old male.}}

